import Native

{-
   Stopping, when something a program promised turns out not to hold.

   A file of its own, and it is worth saying why it was not one: while the prelude was six modules
   these three sat in the container module, because that was the lowest of the six that both targets
   could reach and that every program could see without an import. Being lowest is not what they are
   about, and with the prelude collapsed (Analysis-Modules.md §2.4) nothing is.
-}

{-
   Where a check the compiler inserted goes when it does not hold - Implementation-Containers.md §15.

   In `Core` rather than in `Native` because a check the compiler inserted has to have somewhere to
   land on *both* targets, and half of Native reaches only one of them. Core is implicitly visible
   everywhere, so a check emitted anywhere in the program finds it with no import.

   **No message, and that is deliberate now rather than forced.** This is what every bounds check in
   every program branches to, and printing needs a string, a run and therefore a bounds check of its
   own; a `checkFailed` that formatted would put the formatting machinery in the binary of a program
   that never printed, and would recurse. What each target says is the most its own conventions allow
   with no code behind it - status 134 natively, which is what a process killed by SIGABRT reports,
   and a thrown string on JS, where the emitter supplies the sentence (see NativeOp::HostThrow).
   `assert` below is the form that *does* say something, and it is the one a program writes.

   The compiler calls this rather than a program doing so; `Program::checkFailed` is how it is found.
   Not `pub`, and that is the difference between it and `checkCondition` below: what a check *is* is
   the library's to decide, and a program that reached in and called it would be aborting for a
   reason no check found.
-}
@platform(native) fn checkFailed() -> {} = abortProcess()
@platform(js) fn checkFailed() -> {} = hostFail()

{-
   The same stop, saying where it was - `-check-locations`.

   A second declaration rather than three more parameters on the one above, and that is the whole
   design of the switch: a build without the flag emits calls to `checkCondition`, which has no
   location to carry and no printing behind it, so a program that does not ask pays nothing at all -
   not an argument, not a constant, not a byte of the format machinery. `Program::checkConditionAt`
   is how the compiler finds this one, on the same terms it finds the other.

   **The guard is what makes printing safe here.** The comment above says a `checkFailed` that
   formatted would recurse, and it would: the format path allocates a string, and a string is a run
   with a bounds check of its own. One global says a check has already failed, and a second failure
   reached while reporting the first goes straight to the stop. That is one branch on a path that
   ends the process.
-}
let &reporting = False

{-
   **The file arrives as a pointer and a length, not as a `String`, and that is the whole cost of the
   feature.**

   A native `String` is a two-word descriptor passed by *address*, so a literal one in an argument
   position is sixteen bytes copied into a stack slot **at the call** - which is before the branch,
   on the path every check takes when it holds. Measured on a loop whose bounds check cannot be
   proved away: 30.8 ms became 41.2 ms, a **34%** cost on the passing path, for a message only a
   failing one ever reads. With the same three arguments as scalars it is 30.5 ms, which is the
   plain build to within the noise - the line and column are immediates the register allocator puts
   in the cold arm, and nothing at all lands on the hot one.

   So the two words are passed as two words and the descriptor is rebuilt here, where it is free:
   this body runs once per process and the process is ending. `stringLiteral` is the same
   constructor a written literal goes through.
-}
@platform(native) fn checkFailedAt(file: %CodeUnit, fileLength: Int, line: Int, column: Int) -> {}:
    if reporting then abortProcess()
    reporting = True
    print("check failed at {stringLiteral(file, fileLength)}:{line}:{column}\n")
    abortProcess()

-- On JavaScript a string *is* a host value rather than a descriptor with an address, so passing one
-- costs a register and the pointer form has nothing to buy. The two targets take different arguments
-- for that reason, and `emitCheck` builds whichever this build declared.
@platform(js) fn checkFailedAt(file: String, line: Int, column: Int) -> {}:
    if reporting then hostFail()
    reporting = True
    print("check failed at {file}:{line}:{column}\n")
    hostFail()

-- One located check, with its branch inside it, for the same reason `checkCondition` has one: a
-- subscript is expanded inside whatever expression contains it, and a branch emitted there splits a
-- block underneath a construct that had already decided which blocks it owns.
@platform(native) pub fn checkConditionAt(failed: Bool, file: %CodeUnit, fileLength: Int,
                                          line: Int, column: Int) -> {}:
    if failed then checkFailedAt(file, fileLength, line, column)

@platform(js) pub fn checkConditionAt(failed: Bool, file: String, line: Int, column: Int) -> {}:
    if failed then checkFailedAt(file, line, column)

{-
   One check, with its branch inside it.

   The compiler emits a *call* to this rather than the `if` itself, and that is the whole reason it
   exists: a subscript is expanded inside whatever expression contains it, so a branch emitted there
   splits a block underneath a construct that had already decided which blocks it owns. Here the
   branch is in a function of its own, where nothing is looking. Ordinary inlining removes the call
   in an optimized build; an unoptimized one pays for it, which is the right way round for a check.

   Written as the *mistake* rather than as the invariant - the caller passes `index >= length` - so
   that the branch this contains is predicted the way it will actually go.
-}
pub fn checkCondition(failed: Bool) -> {}:
    if failed then checkFailed()
    return

{-
   The same stop, written the way a program states an invariant rather than the way a compiler emits
   a check - `assert(i < length(xs))` instead of `checkCondition(i >= length(xs))`.

   In the library rather than beside the tests that use it, because an assertion should cost no
   import: Core is implicitly visible everywhere, and what a program is stating here is about its own
   invariants rather than about testing. It is `checkCondition` with the condition the other way
   round and nothing else - the same call, the same inlining, the same status 134 natively and
   the same thrown string on JS.

   **It says what it expected now.** `Show` and the format primitive are files of this module -
   Analysis-Modules.md §2.4 - so an assertion can print, which is the thing the split into
   `Collections` and `Text` made impossible: printing needed `Text`, and `Text` imported the module
   `assert` was in. The message is a defaulted argument rather than a second function, because a
   plain function is one per name per module; an `assert(x)` with no message is the empty string, and
   the branch on its length is what keeps a bare assertion from putting a format in the binary.

   What is still missing is the *source location* and the two values, which no library function can
   reach - `assert(a == b)` sees one `Bool`. That is a compiler-supplied form and belongs with the
   unit-testing framework this is standing in for; the library suite (`test/lib`) is written on the
   bare form, and this becomes the bottom of that rather than the whole of it.

   A caller that stops the process is a caller that has decided the program is wrong, so this is not
   for reporting a failure a program could act on: that is `Result`, and it is what `File` answers.
-}
pub fn assert(held: Bool, message: String = "") -> {}:
    if !held:
        if length(message) > 0 then print("assertion failed: {message}\n")
        checkFailed()

    return

